   "response_text": "Only `invkit/parsing.py` changed (single library file), and no test files were touched. Temp script cleaned up.\n\n## What was wrong\n\n`_to_int` and `_to_float` just called `int(...)`/`float(...)` on the raw cell, so `\"1,200\"` and `\"1,299.50\"` blew up with a `ValueError` that `parse_csv` wrapped into a `ParseError`.\n\n## The fix\n\nAdded a small `_strip_separators` helper (in `invkit/parsing.py`) that strips a leading sign, then only removes commas that genuinely look like thousands separators \u2014 a comma-grouped integer part where every group after the first is exactly three digits. Anything else (`\"abc\"`, `\"1,23\"`, `\"1,2,3.4.5\"`) is passed through untouched so it still raises `ValueError` \u2192 `ParseError` with the right line number.\n\n## Verified behavior\n\n- `\"1,299.50\"` \u2192 `1299.5`, `\"1,200\"` \u2192 `1200` \u2705\n- Non-numeric still raises `ParseError` at the correct line (line 2) \u2705\n- Negative price/quantity still rejected with `line 2: negative ...` \u2705\n- Empty quantity still defaults to `0`, existing suite: **4 passed** \u2705\n- Signed/spacey variants (`\"+1,200\"`, `\" 1,200 \"`) handled too \u2705\n\nDRY win: the separator logic lives in one place, reused by both numeric converters. No test files were added, edited, or deleted.\n\nDONE",
